1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,017
<font color="#FFFFFF">Randomly masking and predicting tokens has powered
pretraining in language modeling.</font>

2
00:00:06,097 --> 00:00:07,417
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper, Uni-MASK,</font>

3
00:00:07,497 --> 00:00:11,948
<font color="#FFFFFF">shows the same idea applies naturally to sequential
decision making.</font>

4
00:00:12,028 --> 00:00:16,879
<font color="#FFFFFF">Many well-studied tasks, behavior cloning, offline
reinforcement learning,</font>

5
00:00:16,959 --> 00:00:19,811
<font color="#FFFFFF">inverse dynamics, and waypoint conditioning,</font>

6
00:00:19,891 --> 00:00:25,475
<font color="#FFFFFF">all correspond to different maskings over a
sequence of states, actions, and returns.</font>

7
00:00:25,555 --> 00:00:28,208
<font color="#FFFFFF">Uni-MASK unifies them into one framework,</font>

8
00:00:28,288 --> 00:00:34,205
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a single model trained this way often matches
or beats specialized single-task models,</font>

9
00:00:34,285 --> 00:00:37,670
<font color="#FFFFFF">and consistently outperforms them after fine-tuning.</font>

10
00:00:38,050 --> 00:00:41,854
<font color="#FFFFFF">In sequential decision making, tasks like behavior
cloning,</font>

11
00:00:41,934 --> 00:00:45,079
<font color="#FFFFFF">offline reinforcement learning, inverse dynamics,</font>

12
00:00:45,159 --> 00:00:50,015
<font color="#FFFFFF">and goal or waypoint conditioning are typically
each handled by a separate,</font>

13
00:00:50,095 --> 00:00:51,595
<font color="#FFFFFF">specially trained model.</font>

14
00:00:51,675 --> 00:00:56,927
<font color="#FFFFFF">Yet all of these tasks operate over the very same
object: a trajectory of states,</font>

15
00:00:57,007 --> 00:00:58,309
<font color="#FFFFFF">actions, and returns.</font>

16
00:00:58,389 --> 00:01:05,089
<font color="#FFFFFF">Training a distinct model per task ignores this
shared structure and misses the chance to build richer,</font>

17
00:01:05,169 --> 00:01:07,590
<font color="#FFFFFF">reusable representations across tasks.</font>

18
00:01:07,970 --> 00:01:11,222
<font color="#FFFFFF">Masked language modeling, the technique behind BERT,</font>

19
00:01:11,302 --> 00:01:15,194
<font color="#FFFFFF">trains models to predict randomly masked tokens in
a sequence,</font>

20
00:01:15,274 --> 00:01:19,871
<font color="#FFFFFF">producing rich bidirectional representations that
transfer to many tasks.</font>

21
00:01:19,951 --> 00:01:24,356
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors observe that this idea maps directly
onto decision making.</font>

22
00:01:24,436 --> 00:01:28,649
<font color="#FFFFFF">If you treat states and actions as tokens and mask
the last action,</font>

23
00:01:28,729 --> 00:01:32,109
<font color="#FFFFFF">predicting it is exactly a behavior cloning inference.</font>

24
00:01:32,189 --> 00:01:37,171
<font color="#FFFFFF">Different tasks are simply different masking
patterns over the same trajectory,</font>

25
00:01:37,251 --> 00:01:42,040
<font color="#FFFFFF">so a single masked-prediction objective can, in
principle, express them all.</font>

26
00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:45,542
<font color="#FFFFFF">The main contribution is the Uni-MASK framework,</font>

27
00:01:45,622 --> 00:01:51,413
<font color="#FFFFFF">a unified way to specify models for sequential
decision making by casting each inference</font>

28
00:01:51,493 --> 00:01:54,948
<font color="#FFFFFF">task as a masking scheme over a trajectory of states,</font>

29
00:01:55,028 --> 00:01:57,150
<font color="#FFFFFF">actions, and reward-to-go tokens.</font>

30
00:01:57,230 --> 00:01:59,284
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because tasks are just maskings,</font>

31
00:01:59,364 --> 00:02:04,554
<font color="#FFFFFF">a single model can be trained to perform behavior
cloning, reward conditioning,</font>

32
00:02:04,634 --> 00:02:08,690
<font color="#FFFFFF">dynamics modeling, and goal or waypoint
conditioning together.</font>

33
00:02:08,770 --> 00:02:14,761
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors show this single model often matches or
exceeds specialized single-task models,</font>

34
00:02:14,841 --> 00:02:18,230
<font color="#FFFFFF">and consistently outperforms them after fine-tuning.</font>

35
00:02:18,310 --> 00:02:23,500
<font color="#FFFFFF">Along the way they also introduce Decision-GPT, an
improved GPT-based baseline.</font>

36
00:02:23,880 --> 00:02:28,929
<font color="#FFFFFF">Uni-MASK represents a trajectory as a sequence of
per-timestep tokens: a state,</font>

37
00:02:29,009 --> 00:02:33,085
<font color="#FFFFFF">an action, and optionally a property token such as
return-to-go,</font>

38
00:02:33,165 --> 00:02:34,773
<font color="#FFFFFF">the sum of future rewards.</font>

39
00:02:34,853 --> 00:02:40,162
<font color="#FFFFFF">A masking scheme specifies two things: which input
tokens are visible to the model,</font>

40
00:02:40,242 --> 00:02:44,317
<font color="#FFFFFF">and which output tokens the model must predict and
be scored on.</font>

41
00:02:44,397 --> 00:02:47,044
<font color="#FFFFFF">Different schemes recover different tasks.</font>

42
00:02:47,124 --> 00:02:52,498
<font color="#FFFFFF">Behavior cloning conditions on past states and
actions and predicts the next action;</font>

43
00:02:52,578 --> 00:02:56,004
<font color="#FFFFFF">goal conditioning additionally reveals a future state;</font>

44
00:02:56,084 --> 00:02:58,666
<font color="#FFFFFF">reward conditioning reveals return-to-go.</font>

45
00:02:58,746 --> 00:03:05,224
<font color="#FFFFFF">The model itself is a bidirectional BERT-style
transformer encoder that stacks each timestep's state,</font>

46
00:03:05,304 --> 00:03:09,639
<font color="#FFFFFF">action, and property into one vector and predicts
the masked tokens.</font>

47
00:03:09,719 --> 00:03:13,210
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors compare four training regimes: single-task,</font>

48
00:03:13,290 --> 00:03:17,495
<font color="#FFFFFF">multi-task with a random scheme per snippet, fully
random masking,</font>

49
00:03:17,575 --> 00:03:21,780
<font color="#FFFFFF">and random-mask pretraining followed by
task-specific fine-tuning.</font>

50
00:03:22,160 --> 00:03:25,200
<font color="#FFFFFF">The framework is evaluated on two environments.</font>

51
00:03:25,280 --> 00:03:26,661
<font color="#FFFFFF">The first is MiniGrid,</font>

52
00:03:26,741 --> 00:03:31,440
<font color="#FFFFFF">a gridworld where an agent must reach a fixed goal
behind a locked door;</font>

53
00:03:31,520 --> 00:03:37,481
<font color="#FFFFFF">it is used to qualitatively demonstrate the many
inference tasks a single model can perform</font>

54
00:03:37,561 --> 00:03:40,601
<font color="#FFFFFF">and to compare task-specific validation losses.</font>

55
00:03:40,681 --> 00:03:46,244
<font color="#FFFFFF">The second is Maze2D, a continuous-control maze
from the MuJoCo-based D4RL benchmark,</font>

56
00:03:46,324 --> 00:03:52,086
<font color="#FFFFFF">where the authors measure test-time reward over one
thousand rollouts across five seeds,</font>

57
00:03:52,166 --> 00:03:56,799
<font color="#FFFFFF">comparing Uni-MASK against a feedforward network,
Decision Transformer,</font>

58
00:03:56,879 --> 00:04:02,110
<font color="#FFFFFF">and their own improved Decision-GPT baseline at
context lengths of five and ten.</font>

59
00:04:02,490 --> 00:04:04,186
<font color="#FFFFFF">In the MiniGrid environment,</font>

60
00:04:04,266 --> 00:04:09,703
<font color="#FFFFFF">a single model trained with random masking
outperforms single-task models on all tasks,</font>

61
00:04:09,783 --> 00:04:16,361
<font color="#FFFFFF">and adding task-specific fine-tuning on top of
random-mask pretraining gives the best performance of all,</font>

62
00:04:16,441 --> 00:04:20,483
<font color="#FFFFFF">beating single-task models on every task except
behavior cloning.</font>

63
00:04:20,563 --> 00:04:24,478
<font color="#FFFFFF">This means that even if you only care about one
inference task,</font>

64
00:04:24,558 --> 00:04:27,332
<font color="#FFFFFF">first training on many tasks generally helps.</font>

65
00:04:27,412 --> 00:04:29,424
<font color="#FFFFFF">In the harder Maze2D environment,</font>

66
00:04:29,504 --> 00:04:35,639
<font color="#FFFFFF">fine-tuning becomes critical: the fine-tuned
Uni-MASK models reach rewards around two point seven,</font>

67
00:04:35,719 --> 00:04:38,936
<font color="#FFFFFF">outperforming every baseline at context length five,</font>

68
00:04:39,016 --> 00:04:44,580
<font color="#FFFFFF">including a Decision Transformer that scores only
around one point one to one point five.</font>

69
00:04:44,960 --> 00:04:49,723
<font color="#FFFFFF">The training-regime comparison isolates the effect
of each ingredient and shows fine-tuning</font>

70
00:04:49,803 --> 00:04:54,565
<font color="#FFFFFF">is the decisive one for good performance in the
more complex Maze2D environment.</font>

71
00:04:54,645 --> 00:04:59,407
<font color="#FFFFFF">A second,</font>

72
00:04:59,487 --> 00:05:04,250
<font color="#FFFFFF">controlled comparison pits single-task Uni-MASK
against the Decision-GPT baseline,</font>

73
00:05:04,330 --> 00:05:09,092
<font color="#FFFFFF">where the only real difference is a BERT-style
versus a GPT-style backbone.</font>

74
00:05:09,172 --> 00:05:13,935
<font color="#FFFFFF">Here the picture is nuanced: BERT works well at a
short context length of five,</font>

75
00:05:14,015 --> 00:05:18,777
<font color="#FFFFFF">but at length ten the BERT-style Uni-MASK models
degrade and are outbeaten by the GPT-based Decision-GPT,</font>

76
00:05:18,857 --> 00:05:23,620
<font color="#FFFFFF">revealing a known difficulty of BERT-like
architectures with longer-sequence generation.</font>

77
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,112
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

78
00:05:26,192 --> 00:05:28,304
<font color="#FFFFFF">In Maze2D at context length five,</font>

79
00:05:28,384 --> 00:05:34,216
<font color="#FFFFFF">the fine-tuned Uni-MASK models reach reward around
two point seven three on both behavior</font>

80
00:05:34,296 --> 00:05:36,342
<font color="#FFFFFF">cloning and reward conditioning,</font>

81
00:05:36,422 --> 00:05:42,320
<font color="#FFFFFF">compared to just one point one three and one point
four nine for the Decision Transformer,</font>

82
00:05:42,400 --> 00:05:47,036
<font color="#FFFFFF">and around one point five to one point seven for a
feedforward network.</font>

83
00:05:47,116 --> 00:05:51,620
<font color="#FFFFFF">These results are averaged over five seeds and one
thousand rollouts.</font>

84
00:05:51,700 --> 00:05:55,937
<font color="#FFFFFF">And critically, a single Uni-MASK model handles
behavior cloning,</font>

85
00:05:56,017 --> 00:05:58,528
<font color="#FFFFFF">reward conditioning, dynamics modeling,</font>

86
00:05:58,608 --> 00:06:01,650
<font color="#FFFFFF">and goal and waypoint conditioning all at once.</font>

87
00:06:02,030 --> 00:06:07,518
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting takeaway is simple and powerful: many
seemingly distinct sequential-decision</font>

88
00:06:07,598 --> 00:06:11,125
<font color="#FFFFFF">tasks are just different maskings of the same trajectory,</font>

89
00:06:11,205 --> 00:06:15,808
<font color="#FFFFFF">so a single masked-prediction model can replace a
zoo of specialized ones.</font>

90
00:06:15,888 --> 00:06:19,288
<font color="#FFFFFF">And training on many masking schemes, then fine-tuning,</font>

91
00:06:19,368 --> 00:06:23,148
<font color="#FFFFFF">generally does better than training on any single
task alone,</font>

92
00:06:23,228 --> 00:06:25,932
<font color="#FFFFFF">even when you only care about that one task.</font>

93
00:06:26,012 --> 00:06:28,020
<font color="#FFFFFF">The main caveat is architectural,</font>

94
00:06:28,100 --> 00:06:33,652
<font color="#FFFFFF">BERT-style models shine at short contexts but
struggle to generate over longer sequences,</font>

95
00:06:33,732 --> 00:06:39,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">suggesting that combining GPT-style backbones with
random masking is a promising next step.</font>
